+| src/repl/render.rs | (round 4, in the row above) | **`.pretty` spells out the characters a terminal acts on, and `.json` does not — and says so.** A right-to-left override or a zero-width mark is written `\u202e` in the document `.pretty` writes: JSON's own escape, so the document still reads back through `json.parse` as the value it came from. `.json` keeps the language's own bytes, because the document it prints is the document `json.stringify` writes, byte for byte, and a document that differed from the language's own writer would be a document no program could produce. SPEC.md §"json and csv" now says which is which → FINDINGS.md §6 |
+| src/repl/render.rs, src/repl/mod.rs, tests/repl_printing_test.rs | (round 4, in the row above) | **the identity is pinned by tests, not by prose.** `edge_the_json_document_is_the_language_s_own_writer_s_document` holds the renderer against the language's own writer, and `edge_the_json_a_session_prints_is_the_document_json_stringify_writes` holds it against the language's own `json.stringify` inside a real session: the same value, printed twice, byte for byte the same. A change to the language writer's shape (`, ` → `,`) now fails a test instead of silently making the two documents differ |
