+## 6. `.json` writes the characters a terminal acts on, raw
+
+**Where:** `src/repl/render.rs` — `write_json`, which writes a text leaf
+through `crate::runtime::json_stringify` (`src/runtime.rs`), and `json_text`,
+which is the writer `.pretty` uses instead.
+
+**What:** `.pretty` and `.table` spell out the characters a terminal acts on
+— the C1 controls, the bidi and zero-width marks — and `.json` does not: a
+text holding a right-to-left override is written as the value holds it, so
+`.json "\u202eabc"` puts a raw override on the terminal and reverses the line.
+The table's cell and the pretty document both avoid it; the document does
+not.
+
+**Why it was not done here:** `.json` is the document `json.stringify` writes,
+byte for byte, and that identity is the command's whole claim: a reader who
+copies the document into a program gets the same document back. Escaping the
+marks in the REPL alone would make the two documents differ; escaping them in
+the language's own writer would change what every program that calls
+`json.stringify` gets, which is the language's surface and not this phase's —
+the same argument as §5. So `.json` is a document first and a terminal
+rendering second, and SPEC.md §"json and csv" says so in as many words.
+
+**A phase would:** decide what the language's `json.stringify` does about the
+characters a terminal acts on — escape them in the language's own writer, so
+that `.json` and the language's document are one document and neither of them
+mangles a terminal — and write that decision down in SPEC.md before writing
+the code.

